Local Variables vs Instance Variables:

Local Variables: These variables are found within a method and cannot be accessed outside from the method. 
Once the method has completed its tasks, memory to the variables will be deallocated.

Instance variables (Fields): These are variables found within a class, but are outside the class's methods. 
These variables hold attributes of an object. Every instance of an object will hold a unique copy of its instance variables.
Instance variables are usually private because of the sensitivity of its information. To access them, you would need a set method and a get method.
Set methods (modifiers) allow you to change the information of an instance variable.
Get methods (access) allow you to get an instance variable's information.
For instance variables, Java provides a default value so you do not need to initialize them.
